      "id": 1,
      "text": "Harrisonville earned its name in 1840 when settlers renamed the crossroads after President William Henry Harrison, the ninth U.S. president.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/harrisonville_pennsylvania",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In the 1850s, Harrisonville boasted two stores, a schoolhouse, a hotel, a blacksmith shop, and only seven residents.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harrisonville%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Harrisonville experiences a humid continental climate with cold winters and mild summers, typical of the Dfb/Dfa Köppen classification.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/harrisonville_pennsylvania",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Pennsylvania Route 655, which cuts through Harrisonville, follows the ancient Warriors Path, a Native American trail that once linked the Potomac River to central Pennsylvania.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/harrisonville_pennsylvania",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Licking Creek, a major southward‑flowing tributary of the Potomac River, borders Harrisonville and shapes the valley’s drainage patterns.",
